- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): This depends on both the intrinsic brightness and the distance of the star. Alone, it is not a good indicator of giant status, but very bright apparent stars could be giants or nearby dwarfs.  
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star. Giants tend to be luminous and thus can be seen at larger distances with smaller parallax.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Large error reduces confidence in distance and absolute magnitude calculation, making classification harder.  
- B-V (color index): Giants generally have characteristic color indices depending on spectral type; they tend to be redder (higher B-V) than dwarfs of the same spectral class.  
- SpType (spectral type): Luminosity class in spectral type is a key indicator (e.g., III or IV indicates giant or subgiant), while V indicates dwarf.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have brighter (lower) absolute magnitudes compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.  

